# Mutation plan for the four image routes — `POST` and `DELETE
# /api/v4/users/{user_id}/image`, `GET /api/v4/users/{user_id}/image/default` and
# `POST /api/v4/brand/image`.
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/image-writes.plan
#
# Every success on these four routes is Go's, so what is under test is the refusals and the
# *position* of each hand-over. Three populations.
#
# The twenty-three **api** mutations run against `parity::image_writes`. Half of them are check
# order rather than check direction, because order is what this family gets wrong: the storage
# check sits in a different place on each of the three writes, the brand route tests `edit_brand`
# fourth, and the same failed `GetUser` is a 400 on one route and a 404 on its neighbour. Four
# more are the two size limits — 512 bytes apart, each with a row on either side of the boundary,
# which is only reachable at all because `both_size_limits_are_where_go_puts_them` runs against a
# second mm-api with the cap at 1 KiB.
#
# The four **router** mutations exist because a route registered on the wrong method, or one
# segment short, still answers — from Go, byte-identically. Only `x-mmrs-served-by` can tell.
#
# The five **app** mutations are `IsProfileImageLockedForUser`'s four conjuncts and
# `SaveBrandImage`'s guard, none of which is reachable over REST on an unlicensed stack with a
# file driver configured.
#
# The two controls are a closure-binding rename in `declared_content_length` and a reorder of two
# independent struct-literal fields in the environment overlay. If either comes back CAUGHT the
# harness is deciding verdicts on something other than the mutation and the whole batch is void.

